  "ai_overview": {
    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Neither SGLT2 inhibitors nor GLP-1 receptor agonists have completely replaced metformin as the universal first-line therapy for type 2 diabetes, though guidelines from groups like the American Diabetes Association (ADA) recommend them as direct first-line or early add-on options for patients with specific cardiorenal risks.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "Neither SGLT2 inhibitors nor GLP-1 receptor agonists have completely replaced metformin as the universal first-line therapy for type 2 diabetes"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Choosing between an SGLT2 inhibitor and a GLP-1 receptor agonist depends heavily on individual patient organ protection needs and side effect profiles:"
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "SGLT2 Inhibitors (Oral)"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Primary Strengths: Superior for heart failure (reducing hospitalizations and cardiac outcomes) and chronic kidney disease (CKD) (slowing progression of renal decline).",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              2
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Formulation: Mostly taken as a daily pill (oral).",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Other Benefits: Moderate weight loss and blood pressure reduction.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              3
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Key Risks/Considerations: Risk of genital fungal infections, urinary tract infections, volume depletion, and rare diabetic ketoacidosis; less potent for lowering HbA1c than GLP-1s.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              4,
              5
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "GLP-1 Receptor Agonists (Mostly Injectable)"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Primary Strengths: Superior for HbA1c/blood sugar lowering, weight loss , and stroke prevention (ASCVD). Oral semaglutide (Rybelsus) is a pill option, though most are weekly injections.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              2,
              4,
              5
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Formulation: Mostly weekly subcutaneous injections, with oral options available.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              2
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Other Benefits: Significant cardiovascular risk reduction and emerging renal benefits.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              6
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Key Risks/Considerations: Gastrointestinal side effects (nausea, vomiting, diarrhea) and a contraindication for individuals with a personal or family history of medullary thyroid carcinoma or Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia type 2 (MEN 2).",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Head-to-Head Comparison & Combination Therapy"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "MACE/Cardiovascular Outcomes: Major adverse cardiovascular event (MACE) reduction is comparable between both classes."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Combination Therapy: If cost and insurance allow, using both agents together provides complementary benefits for glycemic control, weight, heart, and kidney health."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Learn more about clinical guidelines via the American College of Cardiology .",
            "snippet_links": [
              {
                "text": "American College of Cardiology",
                "link": "https://www.acc.org/latest-in-cardiology/ten-points-to-remember/2021/07/09/15/27/sglt2-inhibitors-and-glp1"
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              6,
              7
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "To help narrow down which option fits best, let me know:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Does the patient have heart failure, kidney disease, or a history of stroke/heart attack ?"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Is weight loss or daily/weekly dosing preference a major factor?"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
